WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.322 --> 00:00:09.739
- Thank you very much, Bobby.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.597
I had a similar situation when I

00:00:16.656 --> 00:00:18.978
was working at the British Library.

00:00:18.978 --> 00:00:21.221
We'd just finished the
Centre for Conservation,

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:22.958
a $25 million project.

00:00:22.958 --> 00:00:25.530
It was such a celebration.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.519
The night before, the architects

00:00:28.519 --> 00:00:31.374
of the British Library
died, but we decided

00:00:31.374 --> 00:00:33.101
to go ahead with it, the celebration,

00:00:33.101 --> 00:00:35.101
because in honor of him.

00:00:36.677 --> 00:00:39.142
When Bobby told me the news just earlier,

00:00:39.142 --> 00:00:41.354
we decided to go ahead with the talk

00:00:41.354 --> 00:00:43.409
in Frank's honor.

00:00:43.409 --> 00:00:45.961
I would have been
meeting with him tomorrow

00:00:45.961 --> 00:00:47.479
as two newbies.

00:00:47.479 --> 00:00:49.873
He was two months into being the

00:00:49.873 --> 00:00:52.068
university librarian, and I'm six months

00:00:52.068 --> 00:00:56.582
into being deputy director of
Harvard University Library.

00:00:56.582 --> 00:00:57.832
We'll go ahead.

00:01:00.248 --> 00:01:02.665
So, this is a talk that Bobby

00:01:05.473 --> 00:01:08.496
invited me to give about a year ago.

00:01:08.496 --> 00:01:11.846
It's an enormous privilege and honor

00:01:11.846 --> 00:01:14.429
to be invited to give the talk.

00:01:16.526 --> 00:01:17.359
Start off.

00:01:18.935 --> 00:01:22.185
We'll turn the lights down in a second.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.527
It's a privilege to be giving it

00:01:25.527 --> 00:01:28.369
to this audience because
I was thinking about it.

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:30.836
There's only a couple of things to which

00:01:30.836 --> 00:01:32.680
I think I can lay claim.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.074
One of them is that I'm guessing that I'm

00:01:35.074 --> 00:01:38.324
the only Brother Helen in the audience.

00:01:39.475 --> 00:01:43.120
The reason that I'm Brother Helen is that

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.943
I'm an associate of the Artworkers Guild,

00:01:45.943 --> 00:01:48.937
which was set up in the UK during

00:01:48.937 --> 00:01:51.187
the Arts and Crafts movement.

00:01:51.187 --> 00:01:55.110
We're all brethren,
hence the Brother Helen.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.510
I was taken there and introduced to it

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:01.954
by Roger Powell, who
rebound the Book of Kells,

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:04.270
together with his partner,
Douglas Cockerell,

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:07.246
he rebound the Codex Sinaiticus,

00:02:07.246 --> 00:02:09.054
and his son Sandy Cockerell.

00:02:09.054 --> 00:02:12.942
They laid the foundation
of book and manuscript

00:02:12.942 --> 00:02:14.708
conservation, I think, in the UK.

00:02:14.708 --> 00:02:16.938
Their influence has traveled very widely.

00:02:16.938 --> 00:02:21.105
For example, Peter Waters
at the Library of Congress.

00:02:22.001 --> 00:02:26.119
Well, Bobby invited me to
give this talk last year.

00:02:26.119 --> 00:02:29.717
I think I'm still slightly surprised

00:02:29.717 --> 00:02:31.399
to have taken the train this morning

00:02:31.399 --> 00:02:33.562
from Boston instead of having flown across

00:02:33.562 --> 00:02:34.729
from Heathrow.

00:02:35.781 --> 00:02:38.741
This time last year,
when Bobby invited me,

00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:40.744
I was at the British
Library, where I was part

00:02:40.744 --> 00:02:42.195
of the senior leadership team, and I

00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:44.578
was head of collection care.

00:02:44.578 --> 00:02:45.984
I was responsible for the care

00:02:45.984 --> 00:02:49.148
of 115 million items and 300 terabytes

00:02:49.148 --> 00:02:51.028
of electronic content.

00:02:51.028 --> 00:02:53.459
Those collections and
that content, it ranged

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:56.058
from medieval manuscripts in papyri

00:02:56.058 --> 00:02:59.134
and oracle bones, through
to literary authors' emails

00:02:59.134 --> 00:03:02.565
and websites of the UK web domain.

00:03:02.565 --> 00:03:05.422
In my time at the BL, we completed

00:03:05.422 --> 00:03:07.935
the Centre for Conservation.

00:03:07.935 --> 00:03:11.710
We'd recently finished the 135 miles

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:15.097
of storage, of a storage building

00:03:15.097 --> 00:03:17.951
that built on the Yale-Harvard model.

00:03:17.951 --> 00:03:22.801
High bay, high density,
robotic, low-oxygen storage.

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:25.735
1/2 a mile of stock a day was being moved

00:03:25.735 --> 00:03:27.318
into that facility.

00:03:28.439 --> 00:03:30.355
I was involved with a newspaper strategy,

00:03:30.355 --> 00:03:33.211
which was dealing with 750 million pages

00:03:33.211 --> 00:03:35.044
of physical newsprint.

00:03:36.383 --> 00:03:38.987
The digital library system was underway.

00:03:38.987 --> 00:03:42.504
I'd just helped establish
the digital preservation.

00:03:42.504 --> 00:03:45.809
That went from seven years
ago, when there was not

00:03:45.809 --> 00:03:47.995
a single digital preservation professional

00:03:47.995 --> 00:03:49.862
in a UK library or archive,

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:52.224
and I had to poach from Australia,

00:03:52.224 --> 00:03:56.783
to when I left, we'd
then just had a retreat.

00:03:56.783 --> 00:03:58.745
Around the table, there
was now a hundred years

00:03:58.745 --> 00:04:02.140
of experience in digital preservation.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:05.092
I was also just finishing
the stewardship strategy

00:04:05.092 --> 00:04:06.731
for the British Library's physical

00:04:06.731 --> 00:04:09.959
and virtual collections
for the next 10 years.

00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:12.149
That was a year ago when Bobby invited me

00:04:12.149 --> 00:04:13.554
to give the talk.

00:04:13.554 --> 00:04:15.756
Scroll forward a year, and I've now

00:04:15.756 --> 00:04:18.389
been at Harvard six months.

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:20.855
I'm currently responsible for the programs

00:04:20.855 --> 00:04:24.192
across the system of 73 libraries.

00:04:24.192 --> 00:04:26.249
That's information systems, archives

00:04:26.249 --> 00:04:28.926
and records management,
preservation, conservation,

00:04:28.926 --> 00:04:31.093
high-density store and the

00:04:32.335 --> 00:04:35.555
Office for Scholarly Communications,

00:04:35.555 --> 00:04:38.179
which champions open access.

00:04:38.179 --> 00:04:41.191
These provide the services
across the library system,

00:04:41.191 --> 00:04:45.358
which you probably know is
a very decentralized system.

00:04:46.302 --> 00:04:49.198
Plus, you may also know that at Harvard

00:04:49.198 --> 00:04:52.404
last year, the president
and provost instigated

00:04:52.404 --> 00:04:54.819
a task force to look at the future

00:04:54.819 --> 00:04:57.329
of the entire 73-library system,

00:04:57.329 --> 00:04:58.742
from a governance of funding

00:04:58.742 --> 00:05:01.208
and a structural perspective.

00:05:01.208 --> 00:05:05.014
On my second day, I joined
the task force's work.

00:05:05.014 --> 00:05:06.795
It's looking at the strategic future

00:05:06.795 --> 00:05:08.518
of the library system.

00:05:08.518 --> 00:05:10.921
It's due to report in the near future,

00:05:10.921 --> 00:05:13.239
and so it wouldn't be appropriate for me

00:05:13.239 --> 00:05:16.494
to comment on that major
fundamental review now,

00:05:16.494 --> 00:05:19.172
but there are already key directions

00:05:19.172 --> 00:05:20.706
that came out clearly.

00:05:20.706 --> 00:05:23.066
For example, working towards one

00:05:23.066 --> 00:05:26.712
integrated collection
and being user-centric,

00:05:26.712 --> 00:05:30.129
or putting the user as our primary focus.

00:05:31.072 --> 00:05:32.739
Well, for this talk,

00:05:34.553 --> 00:05:36.973
I'm going to pick out
five or six paradoxes

00:05:36.973 --> 00:05:39.494
that I've been pondering
around preservation,

00:05:39.494 --> 00:05:42.914
even though it's now not my primary focus.

00:05:42.914 --> 00:05:45.218
As this is billed as a
personal and strategic

00:05:45.218 --> 00:05:48.716
perspective, it's my take on things

00:05:48.716 --> 00:05:50.658
through the lens of having just moved

00:05:50.658 --> 00:05:53.325
3,276 miles and through the lens

00:05:54.575 --> 00:05:58.137
of having journeyed from
an artisan conservation

00:05:58.137 --> 00:06:00.778
to strategic management.

00:06:00.778 --> 00:06:02.435
I'm going to use a couple of examples

00:06:02.435 --> 00:06:04.152
from the British Library
because you probably

00:06:04.152 --> 00:06:06.211
already know a lot about Harvard,

00:06:06.211 --> 00:06:09.628
and may not be so acquainted with the BL.

00:06:10.897 --> 00:06:12.672
A year ago, I was working
at the British Library,

00:06:12.672 --> 00:06:14.772
one of the greatest research
libraries in the world,

00:06:14.772 --> 00:06:16.410
and now I'm at Harvard,
one of the greatest

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.615
research libraries in the world.

00:06:18.615 --> 00:06:20.058
The British Library now has a

00:06:20.058 --> 00:06:22.416
well-integrated preservation
and conservation setup,

00:06:22.416 --> 00:06:24.778
with which I'm extremely proud.

00:06:24.778 --> 00:06:27.433
Harvard has an extremely
impressive preservation

00:06:27.433 --> 00:06:29.424
and conservation setup.

00:06:29.424 --> 00:06:31.538
On a personal note,
I'd like to pay tribute

00:06:31.538 --> 00:06:35.211
to Jan Merrill-Oldham,
who retired last month.

00:06:35.211 --> 00:06:38.336
Her forethought and very wise decisions

00:06:38.336 --> 00:06:41.761
have created a very
professional department.

00:06:41.761 --> 00:06:43.962
As an aside, if anyone wants to talk to me

00:06:43.962 --> 00:06:45.965
at the reception, we'll be recruiting

00:06:45.965 --> 00:06:50.509
to that post later in the year. (laughter)

00:06:50.509 --> 00:06:52.496
The British Library has a stupendous

00:06:52.496 --> 00:06:57.247
and slightly irrational
legacy from over 250 years.

00:06:57.247 --> 00:07:00.094
Harvard has a phenomenal historical legacy

00:07:00.094 --> 00:07:01.594
of over 350 years.

00:07:03.544 --> 00:07:06.112
They both have magnificent pasts.

00:07:06.112 --> 00:07:09.753
I'll start with a very
obvious, simple paradox.

00:07:09.753 --> 00:07:11.763
Namely, that people
often think preservation

00:07:11.763 --> 00:07:13.820
is all about the past, but to my mind,

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:15.899
it's all about the future.

00:07:15.899 --> 00:07:18.267
It's about anticipating the future

00:07:18.267 --> 00:07:20.657
rather than adhering to the past,

00:07:20.657 --> 00:07:24.327
and being able to look
a user of the future

00:07:24.327 --> 00:07:26.356
in the metaphorical eye and say,

00:07:26.356 --> 00:07:29.023
"Well, we did our best
to meet present need

00:07:29.023 --> 00:07:31.239
"and anticipate your needs."

00:07:31.239 --> 00:07:33.345
That's also the user-centricity that

00:07:33.345 --> 00:07:37.264
Harvard's task force is talking about.

00:07:37.264 --> 00:07:41.694
From a simple one,
through the next paradox.

00:07:41.694 --> 00:07:44.944
There's been lots of talk that the rise

00:07:46.407 --> 00:07:48.855
in digital initiatives leads to the demise

00:07:48.855 --> 00:07:51.249
of the book, and a lot of anxiety that

00:07:51.249 --> 00:07:52.971
that leads to a demise in the need

00:07:52.971 --> 00:07:55.148
for preservation.

00:07:55.148 --> 00:07:58.218
Paradoxically, not
necessarily, and whatever,

00:07:58.218 --> 00:08:00.307
it's not that simple.

00:08:00.307 --> 00:08:03.149
Yes, last year, oh, excuse me, last week,

00:08:03.149 --> 00:08:07.779
Amazon said it sold more
e-books on amazon.com

00:08:07.779 --> 00:08:11.417
during the previous 30
days than print books,

00:08:11.417 --> 00:08:14.546
that's both hardcover
and paperback combined,

00:08:14.546 --> 00:08:16.546
of its best seller list.

00:08:17.451 --> 00:08:20.056
Yes, we're seeing the complete
life cycle of the media.

00:08:20.056 --> 00:08:23.008
Did you see that Sony
this week, last week,

00:08:23.008 --> 00:08:26.486
actually, it stopped
making its Walkman tapes.

00:08:26.486 --> 00:08:30.594
That's a 30-year full
life cycle come and gone.

00:08:30.594 --> 00:08:33.881
Yes, Generation X, Generation
Y and Generation Zed,

00:08:33.881 --> 00:08:36.617
all those digital natives, they have got

00:08:36.617 --> 00:08:39.284
expectations of access to everything

00:08:39.284 --> 00:08:42.284
at any time and any place, anywhere.

00:08:43.242 --> 00:08:46.856
Brewster Kahle, the internet archiver,

00:08:46.856 --> 00:08:50.772
he recently estimated that
a hundred million books

00:08:50.772 --> 00:08:53.359
will be disposed of across the US

00:08:53.359 --> 00:08:55.800
in the next 10 years.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.090
These are not unique items, but if

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:00.730
they're disposed of,
ironically, they might

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:02.814
become more special.

00:09:02.814 --> 00:09:04.381
So here's the paradox.

00:09:04.381 --> 00:09:07.167
Brewster Kahle, one of the earliest people

00:09:07.167 --> 00:09:09.777
to realize the fragility of websites

00:09:09.777 --> 00:09:11.691
and do something about it by setting up

00:09:11.691 --> 00:09:15.535
the Wayback Machine and
the Internet Archive

00:09:15.535 --> 00:09:18.109
in order to preserve the web, so he,

00:09:18.109 --> 00:09:20.461
quintessential internet archiver,

00:09:20.461 --> 00:09:23.452
is setting up a physical repository.

00:09:23.452 --> 00:09:27.368
He's welcoming and will
store discarded copies

00:09:27.368 --> 00:09:30.285
as everyone digitizes and discards.

00:09:31.607 --> 00:09:33.585
What the Brewster Kahle
example illustrates

00:09:33.585 --> 00:09:35.238
is that the physical is taking on

00:09:35.238 --> 00:09:38.292
greater significance when
its existence is threatened,

00:09:38.292 --> 00:09:40.108
which is leading to a greater emphasis

00:09:40.108 --> 00:09:42.902
on preservation, ironically, where there

00:09:42.902 --> 00:09:46.985
has been concern that
preservation is threatened.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:49.791
I'm going to read you a quote taken from

00:09:49.791 --> 00:09:52.269
last month's launch of the
British Library's vision

00:09:52.269 --> 00:09:53.102
for 2020.

00:09:54.762 --> 00:09:58.650
To put this in context,
this strategic vision

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.784
was posted just ahead of the comprehensive

00:10:00.784 --> 00:10:03.911
spending review, which
was a fundamental review

00:10:03.911 --> 00:10:07.917
of all federal funding,
all federal spending

00:10:07.917 --> 00:10:09.886
by the new coalition government.

00:10:09.886 --> 00:10:11.655
It's looking at everything from defense

00:10:11.655 --> 00:10:13.757
to education to health.

00:10:13.757 --> 00:10:16.732
In the event, it made 19% cuts

00:10:16.732 --> 00:10:19.712
in government spending
across the whole of the UK.

00:10:19.712 --> 00:10:23.492
My colleague at the BL,
who'd been profiling

00:10:23.492 --> 00:10:27.659
25% and 40% cuts, she
made the wry comment,

00:10:28.659 --> 00:10:30.543
"I never thought I'd say, Helen,

00:10:30.543 --> 00:10:33.851
"that 15% would be a good result."

00:10:33.851 --> 00:10:37.268
Anyway, this is the BL's vision for 2020.

00:10:38.609 --> 00:10:41.859
Could you turn the lights down, please?

00:10:44.447 --> 00:10:49.247
"We estimate that by
2020, 75% of all titles

00:10:49.247 --> 00:10:52.131
"worldwide will be published
in digital form only,

00:10:52.131 --> 00:10:54.779
"or in both digital and print.

00:10:54.779 --> 00:10:57.139
"Our ambition is to
preserve digital content

00:10:57.139 --> 00:10:58.972
"for the long term, in order to safeguard

00:10:58.972 --> 00:11:01.118
"our intellectual heritage
so that it can be used

00:11:01.118 --> 00:11:04.103
"by future generations of researchers.

00:11:04.103 --> 00:11:06.404
"As digital formats become the norm,

00:11:06.404 --> 00:11:08.461
"our rich resource of physical content

00:11:08.461 --> 00:11:11.449
"will become more precious.

00:11:11.449 --> 00:11:14.337
"It is thus vital that
we continue to develop

00:11:14.337 --> 00:11:16.454
"our world-class stewardship skills

00:11:16.454 --> 00:11:19.671
"in conservation and preservation."

00:11:19.671 --> 00:11:22.439
Paradoxically, more preservation

00:11:22.439 --> 00:11:25.605
of more precious physical material

00:11:25.605 --> 00:11:27.425
whilst also there's obviously the rise

00:11:27.425 --> 00:11:29.682
in digital preservation.

00:11:29.682 --> 00:11:32.893
This from an organization
that is absolutely

00:11:32.893 --> 00:11:34.804
positioning itself in the forefront

00:11:34.804 --> 00:11:36.589
of the digital world.

00:11:36.589 --> 00:11:39.853
The prime minister
launched Digital Britain

00:11:39.853 --> 00:11:44.287
from the British Library
when I was there last year.

00:11:44.287 --> 00:11:46.906
Furthermore, (clears throat) digitization,

00:11:46.906 --> 00:11:49.827
excuse me, (coughs) digitization can lead

00:11:49.827 --> 00:11:51.690
to greater emphasis and importance

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:54.477
on the special physical, not less,

00:11:54.477 --> 00:11:56.765
and virtualization is
leading to the creation

00:11:56.765 --> 00:11:59.505
of new digital entities.

00:11:59.505 --> 00:12:02.395
As the mass digitization initiatives,

00:12:02.395 --> 00:12:04.916
such as Google, and the
boutique digitization

00:12:04.916 --> 00:12:08.069
of small projects start to join up,

00:12:08.069 --> 00:12:10.177
they make for a new critical mass,

00:12:10.177 --> 00:12:13.041
a new massive digital entity that can

00:12:13.041 --> 00:12:16.600
be linked and mined and studied and used

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:19.619
in ways that were
unimaginable, unimaginable,

00:12:19.619 --> 00:12:20.869
until recently.

00:12:22.247 --> 00:12:25.747
I'll illustrate this with the digitization

00:12:27.559 --> 00:12:31.497
and virtual reunification
of the Codex Sinaiticus,

00:12:31.497 --> 00:12:34.782
which is the earliest
existing text in the world

00:12:34.782 --> 00:12:36.532
of the New Testament.

00:12:43.758 --> 00:12:45.852
One of the only other claims I can make

00:12:45.852 --> 00:12:49.138
is that until the 6th of July last year,

00:12:49.138 --> 00:12:52.261
I was one of maybe three people alive

00:12:52.261 --> 00:12:54.670
who had seen all the surviving manuscript

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:59.246
of the Codex Sinaiticus
in the four locations.

00:12:59.246 --> 00:13:02.934
Namely, St. Catherine's
Monastery in Egypt,

00:13:02.934 --> 00:13:05.145
from where the majority was taken

00:13:05.145 --> 00:13:07.737
in the 19th century by Tischendorf,

00:13:07.737 --> 00:13:11.609
to Leipzig in Germany, and then it went

00:13:11.609 --> 00:13:14.692
to St. Petersburg and then to London.

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:20.445
On the 16th, excuse me, on the 6th of July

00:13:20.445 --> 00:13:23.271
last year, the digitized manuscripts

00:13:23.271 --> 00:13:25.938
were brought together virtually.

00:13:26.948 --> 00:13:30.522
Instead of having been
seen by three people

00:13:30.522 --> 00:13:33.189
in the world, on that day alone,

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:37.217
they were viewed by 20 million people.

00:13:38.362 --> 00:13:40.460
The Codex Sinaiticus project digitized

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:41.766
all the leaves and fragments from

00:13:41.766 --> 00:13:43.349
the four locations.

00:13:44.324 --> 00:13:48.443
There are 347 leaves in the
British Library in London,

00:13:48.443 --> 00:13:50.807
bought from the Russian government in 1933

00:13:50.807 --> 00:13:53.536
for the equivalent of about $3 million.

00:13:53.536 --> 00:13:56.309
They arrived, unbound, and were bound

00:13:56.309 --> 00:14:00.142
into two volumes in 1935
by Douglas Cockerell.

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:03.253
When I worked for Roger Powell, he used

00:14:03.253 --> 00:14:06.054
to tell us stories about
Cockerell's conservation

00:14:06.054 --> 00:14:08.126
and binding of the Codex.

00:14:08.126 --> 00:14:10.113
So from a personal point of view,

00:14:10.113 --> 00:14:12.655
there was a lovely circularity in leading

00:14:12.655 --> 00:14:14.891
the conservation of the
Codex Sinaiticus project

00:14:14.891 --> 00:14:19.285
some 60 years later, and
finding in the archives

00:14:19.285 --> 00:14:21.075
all the materials testing and all

00:14:21.075 --> 00:14:24.297
the treatments and all the correspondence

00:14:24.297 --> 00:14:26.714
between Powell and Cockerell.

00:14:28.657 --> 00:14:31.554
Then, there were 43 leaves in

00:14:31.554 --> 00:14:33.668
Leipzig University Library in Germany.

00:14:33.668 --> 00:14:36.793
Those are currently unbound.

00:14:36.793 --> 00:14:38.687
There were four portions of leaves

00:14:38.687 --> 00:14:40.388
in the National Library of Russia

00:14:40.388 --> 00:14:44.695
in St. Petersburg, and
those are currently framed.

00:14:44.695 --> 00:14:48.486
Then there were some or all of 18 leaves

00:14:48.486 --> 00:14:52.773
in St. Catherine's Monastery
in Mount Sinai in Egypt.

00:14:52.773 --> 00:14:54.734
They were recovered by the monks

00:14:54.734 --> 00:14:59.392
from the northern wall
of the monastery in 1975.

00:14:59.392 --> 00:15:00.809
From a conservation point of view,

00:15:00.809 --> 00:15:03.775
they're in the worst condition.

00:15:03.775 --> 00:15:06.324
The project was essentially to digitize

00:15:06.324 --> 00:15:08.717
the leaves and bring the
manuscript back together

00:15:08.717 --> 00:15:11.939
on the web, to reunify it virtually,

00:15:11.939 --> 00:15:14.522
but it was much more than that.

00:15:15.627 --> 00:15:17.865
There was imaging under standard light

00:15:17.865 --> 00:15:19.282
and raking light.

00:15:21.081 --> 00:15:24.359
Then there's the content metadata.

00:15:24.359 --> 00:15:27.189
There's a new transcription.

00:15:27.189 --> 00:15:28.888
It's in Greek.

00:15:28.888 --> 00:15:30.328
The text is in Greek, so there's a new

00:15:30.328 --> 00:15:33.226
transcription into Greek.

00:15:33.226 --> 00:15:35.544
There are translations into modern Greek,

00:15:35.544 --> 00:15:38.384
Russian, German and English.

00:15:38.384 --> 00:15:41.551
Then there's the physical description.

00:15:43.664 --> 00:15:45.274
This physical description was part

00:15:45.274 --> 00:15:47.538
of the conservation
element of the project,

00:15:47.538 --> 00:15:49.087
with major collaboration with the

00:15:49.087 --> 00:15:52.294
curatorial investigations,
with extensive examination

00:15:52.294 --> 00:15:55.585
of the scribe link, the
codicological features,

00:15:55.585 --> 00:15:57.778
the previous repairs,
the current condition,

00:15:57.778 --> 00:16:00.262
the conservation undertaken and all

00:16:00.262 --> 00:16:03.249
the surface characteristics
of the parchment.

00:16:03.249 --> 00:16:05.886
For example, here you can see those curves

00:16:05.886 --> 00:16:07.506
on the left-hand side.

00:16:07.506 --> 00:16:10.133
Those striations made by the,

00:16:10.133 --> 00:16:14.300
thought to be made by the
parchment makers' knives.

00:16:15.187 --> 00:16:18.781
The same documentation
methodology was used

00:16:18.781 --> 00:16:21.948
in each of the four location, and then

00:16:23.523 --> 00:16:25.825
embedded in the website.

00:16:25.825 --> 00:16:28.665
I'm not aware of this level of examination

00:16:28.665 --> 00:16:30.572
being so systematically embedded

00:16:30.572 --> 00:16:32.122
in any other such project.

00:16:32.122 --> 00:16:33.367
It's great to hear, I've just learned

00:16:33.367 --> 00:16:34.824
that it's been shortlisted for the

00:16:34.824 --> 00:16:36.907
UK's Conservation Awards.

00:16:38.247 --> 00:16:40.281
Then there's a whole series of essays

00:16:40.281 --> 00:16:44.448
on the website, which are
being consistently added to.

00:16:46.195 --> 00:16:50.294
Now, this was a seven-year,
$1 1/2 million project.

00:16:50.294 --> 00:16:53.177
The next step was a much shorter,

00:16:53.177 --> 00:16:55.299
less expensive project, to digitize

00:16:55.299 --> 00:16:57.776
all the British Library's
Greek manuscripts,

00:16:57.776 --> 00:16:59.315
building on what was learned from the

00:16:59.315 --> 00:17:03.569
Codex Sinaiticus project
and using crowdsourcing.

00:17:03.569 --> 00:17:06.679
What was being called, as
I left the British Library,

00:17:06.679 --> 00:17:10.076
expertonomies, whereby scholars input

00:17:10.076 --> 00:17:12.522
their research so that it wasn't just

00:17:12.522 --> 00:17:15.669
BL experts writing the text.

00:17:15.669 --> 00:17:18.252
Then there were folksonomies, whereby

00:17:18.252 --> 00:17:20.858
non-scholars could input their,

00:17:20.858 --> 00:17:22.940
so taking it from being a boutique

00:17:22.940 --> 00:17:26.229
digitization project to a wider project

00:17:26.229 --> 00:17:29.673
contributing to this
critical mass of content.

00:17:29.673 --> 00:17:31.197
So here's the thing.

00:17:31.197 --> 00:17:33.569
Here's the paradox.

00:17:33.569 --> 00:17:35.107
Articulated on the day that

00:17:35.107 --> 00:17:37.164
the world's earliest surviving text

00:17:37.164 --> 00:17:39.918
of the New Testament
was virtually reunified,

00:17:39.918 --> 00:17:41.788
on the day that 20 million people

00:17:41.788 --> 00:17:44.950
saw it reunified, on the day that the term

00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:47.437
Codex Sinaiticus was amongst the top 20

00:17:47.437 --> 00:17:50.365
Google searches worldwide and on the day

00:17:50.365 --> 00:17:52.702
that there were over 700 news articles

00:17:52.702 --> 00:17:55.419
about the project, on that day,

00:17:55.419 --> 00:17:58.981
on the 6th of July 2009, on The Guardian

00:17:58.981 --> 00:18:01.995
newspaper's blog, Shirley Dent

00:18:01.995 --> 00:18:05.313
of the Institute of Ideas, she said,

00:18:05.313 --> 00:18:07.638
"We've long been aware that a plethora

00:18:07.638 --> 00:18:09.327
"of literature can be made available

00:18:09.327 --> 00:18:10.967
"through digitized open sources,

00:18:10.967 --> 00:18:12.301
"such as Project Gutenberg.

00:18:12.301 --> 00:18:15.008
"But, ironically, the
potential to make literature

00:18:15.008 --> 00:18:16.918
"available online, in downloadable

00:18:16.918 --> 00:18:19.361
"and now e-book reader format, has also

00:18:19.361 --> 00:18:21.300
"been heralded as the
slow death of the book

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:23.068
"as we know it.

00:18:23.068 --> 00:18:25.388
"What the Codex Sinaiticus
project illustrates

00:18:25.388 --> 00:18:27.865
"is that the opposite could be true.

00:18:27.865 --> 00:18:30.064
"Digital technology may not only preserve

00:18:30.064 --> 00:18:32.095
"hard codices that, even with the best

00:18:32.095 --> 00:18:34.309
"conservation intentions,
will always be vulnerable

00:18:34.309 --> 00:18:36.184
"to the ravages of time.

00:18:36.184 --> 00:18:37.595
"We may in fact be on the brink

00:18:37.595 --> 00:18:40.146
"of digitally resurrecting, reinterpreting

00:18:40.146 --> 00:18:42.729
"and reinvigorating that past."

00:18:46.591 --> 00:18:49.083
My third paradox is around the inevitable

00:18:49.083 --> 00:18:51.616
classic tension between preservation

00:18:51.616 --> 00:18:53.273
and access, which has been debated

00:18:53.273 --> 00:18:55.138
since the beginning of preservation

00:18:55.138 --> 00:18:56.471
as a profession.

00:18:58.232 --> 00:19:00.301
Personally, I've pretty
much changed my view

00:19:00.301 --> 00:19:02.515
since starting as a naive and idealistic

00:19:02.515 --> 00:19:05.155
conservator at the
Victoria and Albert Museum,

00:19:05.155 --> 00:19:07.144
when I first thought
the best way to preserve

00:19:07.144 --> 00:19:09.661
fragile materials was to prevent damage

00:19:09.661 --> 00:19:13.449
by keeping people away from
fragile special collections.

00:19:13.449 --> 00:19:17.119
Now my starting point is how
to make things accessible,

00:19:17.119 --> 00:19:19.803
and then work backwards.

00:19:19.803 --> 00:19:23.463
There's an inherent paradox
in making material available.

00:19:23.463 --> 00:19:26.462
Nicely summarized, I think, in a recent UK

00:19:26.462 --> 00:19:29.379
research project entitled Catch 22.

00:19:32.347 --> 00:19:36.649
The Catch 22 was that
"access to heritage objects

00:19:36.649 --> 00:19:38.994
"brings social benefit.

00:19:38.994 --> 00:19:42.025
"Greater access brings
greater social benefit.

00:19:42.025 --> 00:19:44.537
"Greater access brings greater damage.

00:19:44.537 --> 00:19:48.485
"Greater damage brings
reduced social benefit."

00:19:48.485 --> 00:19:50.276
Access brings social benefit.

00:19:50.276 --> 00:19:52.251
Greater access, greater social benefit.

00:19:52.251 --> 00:19:53.849
Greater access, greater damage.

00:19:53.849 --> 00:19:57.099
Greater damage, reduced social benefit.

00:19:58.944 --> 00:20:01.575
One approach to squaring
this particular circle

00:20:01.575 --> 00:20:04.925
is to take a strategic
management approach.

00:20:04.925 --> 00:20:06.596
Whenever we make material available,

00:20:06.596 --> 00:20:07.996
we're putting it at risk.

00:20:07.996 --> 00:20:10.023
I was very much involved
in collection security

00:20:10.023 --> 00:20:11.356
in my last role.

00:20:12.947 --> 00:20:16.447
Think Forbes Smiley III, Farhad Hakimzade.

00:20:17.799 --> 00:20:20.072
There was a case reported only last week

00:20:20.072 --> 00:20:21.501
of the ex-department head of

00:20:21.501 --> 00:20:23.434
the National Archives here arrested

00:20:23.434 --> 00:20:25.684
in his house by FBI agents.

00:20:27.895 --> 00:20:29.826
But the risk to collections is far wider

00:20:29.826 --> 00:20:31.345
than from theft.

00:20:31.345 --> 00:20:33.435
There's a whole risk
and compliance industry

00:20:33.435 --> 00:20:36.966
that analyzes probability
and severity of risks.

00:20:36.966 --> 00:20:38.885
In the world of conservation
and preservation,

00:20:38.885 --> 00:20:40.714
there are various methods for assessing

00:20:40.714 --> 00:20:42.498
risk of collections.

00:20:42.498 --> 00:20:44.821
For example, that developed at the

00:20:44.821 --> 00:20:46.794
Canadian Museum of Nature.

00:20:46.794 --> 00:20:48.633
Most of you all know this method,

00:20:48.633 --> 00:20:50.683
but it defined agents of deterioration

00:20:50.683 --> 00:20:51.858
and risk factors.

00:20:51.858 --> 00:20:54.433
For example, earthquakes, pests,

00:20:54.433 --> 00:20:56.835
environmental facts, dissociation,

00:20:56.835 --> 00:20:59.992
which is a renaming of that previously

00:20:59.992 --> 00:21:02.213
somewhat pejorative custodial neglect,

00:21:02.213 --> 00:21:04.690
and security and so on.

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:06.945
Ironically, I think an integrated

00:21:06.945 --> 00:21:10.122
risk management approach
encourages innovation

00:21:10.122 --> 00:21:13.354
and entrepreneurship, which
we hear so much about,

00:21:13.354 --> 00:21:16.222
and it encourages risk taking because

00:21:16.222 --> 00:21:19.534
it encourages an organization
to identify risks,

00:21:19.534 --> 00:21:22.507
identify mitigation of risks and actually

00:21:22.507 --> 00:21:25.882
to be explicit about what
its risk appetite is.

00:21:25.882 --> 00:21:27.938
It compels an organization to acknowledge

00:21:27.938 --> 00:21:31.055
what's risky, identify the unknown,

00:21:31.055 --> 00:21:34.141
give a name to fear and fear of failure.

00:21:34.141 --> 00:21:37.724
Then that can help inform your strategy.

00:21:37.724 --> 00:21:39.665
Risk assessments were carried out on all

00:21:39.665 --> 00:21:43.094
the collections of the British Library.

00:21:43.094 --> 00:21:45.909
Here's the British Library.

00:21:45.909 --> 00:21:48.060
There was a risk assessment of all

00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:51.475
the digital collections,
which helped determine

00:21:51.475 --> 00:21:53.536
the digital preservation strategy.

00:21:53.536 --> 00:21:55.839
For example, we discovered
there was already

00:21:55.839 --> 00:21:58.839
a 3% loss of legal deposit CDs.

00:22:01.844 --> 00:22:04.258
Then we did a risk assessment of the

00:22:04.258 --> 00:22:06.841
physical collections, using the

00:22:07.796 --> 00:22:11.129
Canadian Museum of Nature's methodology.

00:22:13.716 --> 00:22:15.406
The two highest risks.

00:22:15.406 --> 00:22:17.265
I was gonna ask what
people thought they were,

00:22:17.265 --> 00:22:21.020
but I can't see anyone,
actually. (laughter)

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:23.781
Would you shout out
(laughs) what do you think

00:22:23.781 --> 00:22:26.187
were the two highest risks of the

00:22:26.187 --> 00:22:30.354
150 million physical items
at the British Library?

00:22:32.834 --> 00:22:34.275
- [Audience Member] Being lost.

00:22:34.275 --> 00:22:36.025
- [Helen] Being lost.

00:22:37.126 --> 00:22:38.280
Any others?

00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:39.831
- [Audience Member] Water damage.

00:22:39.831 --> 00:22:42.280
- [Helen] Water damage.

00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:43.874
Any others?

00:22:43.874 --> 00:22:45.497
- [Audience Member] Terrorism.

00:22:45.497 --> 00:22:47.277
- [Helen] Interesting.

00:22:47.277 --> 00:22:49.009
Actually, if you can see,

00:22:49.009 --> 00:22:50.313
there's the British Library.

00:22:50.313 --> 00:22:52.557
Can you see behind is St. Pancras Station,

00:22:52.557 --> 00:22:54.623
which is the new Eurostar station,

00:22:54.623 --> 00:22:58.354
which is actually an
issue for the security

00:22:58.354 --> 00:23:01.100
of the British Library.

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:03.775
The two highest priority, excuse me,

00:23:03.775 --> 00:23:06.858
the two highest risks were, actually,

00:23:07.912 --> 00:23:11.565
wear and tear, given that 3 million items

00:23:11.565 --> 00:23:15.556
are issued every year
in the reading rooms,

00:23:15.556 --> 00:23:17.849
and security, which is the price

00:23:17.849 --> 00:23:20.567
of giving access to 400,000 visits

00:23:20.567 --> 00:23:23.039
to the reading rooms, but they can

00:23:23.039 --> 00:23:25.741
obviously be mitigated.

00:23:25.741 --> 00:23:27.576
I'd be very interested, actually,

00:23:27.576 --> 00:23:30.239
either in the debates or, excuse me,

00:23:30.239 --> 00:23:32.071
any questions afterwards
or at the reception

00:23:32.071 --> 00:23:35.105
if anyone else has done
these risk assessments

00:23:35.105 --> 00:23:36.721
because I think there's a huge value

00:23:36.721 --> 00:23:38.664
in comparing results.

00:23:38.664 --> 00:23:40.450
I do know of another organization

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:42.698
which have had a very unexpected result,

00:23:42.698 --> 00:23:44.899
which found that one of its major sources

00:23:44.899 --> 00:23:47.523
of risk was from actually having boxed

00:23:47.523 --> 00:23:49.523
so much of its material.

00:23:50.536 --> 00:23:53.369
It was a very high use collection.

00:23:55.802 --> 00:23:58.357
As the material was being transported

00:23:58.357 --> 00:24:01.909
to the reading rooms,
it was getting a lot of,

00:24:01.909 --> 00:24:05.018
it was very fragile material in boxes

00:24:05.018 --> 00:24:07.073
that were too big for it and was actually

00:24:07.073 --> 00:24:09.073
causing a lot of damage.

00:24:11.623 --> 00:24:13.773
If we'd done this risk
assessment 10 years ago

00:24:13.773 --> 00:24:15.921
at the British Library, I'm pretty sure

00:24:15.921 --> 00:24:19.255
that the results would
have been different.

00:24:19.255 --> 00:24:21.273
I think one of the
highest risks would have

00:24:21.273 --> 00:24:23.657
been from environmental factors,

00:24:23.657 --> 00:24:25.680
but since there's been massive improvement

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:28.342
and improved buildings over 15 years

00:24:28.342 --> 00:24:31.592
that will take the BL from before 1998,

00:24:33.358 --> 00:24:36.108
when it moved into that building,

00:24:37.806 --> 00:24:39.653
you could say none of its collections,

00:24:39.653 --> 00:24:41.912
0% of its collections were in satisfactory

00:24:41.912 --> 00:24:44.264
environmental conditions.

00:24:44.264 --> 00:24:46.721
As it moved in there, which was where 1/2

00:24:46.721 --> 00:24:50.299
the collections went, that went to 42%.

00:24:50.299 --> 00:24:53.285
Then with the completion
of the massive new

00:24:53.285 --> 00:24:55.926
storage building, when that's full,

00:24:55.926 --> 00:24:58.796
the new density store is full is 2011,

00:24:58.796 --> 00:25:00.737
it'll go to 66%.

00:25:00.737 --> 00:25:03.993
Over 15 years, 2/3 of the collections

00:25:03.993 --> 00:25:06.046
will have gone into satisfactory

00:25:06.046 --> 00:25:07.751
environmental conditions.

00:25:07.751 --> 00:25:09.730
That minimizes that risk.

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:12.055
I know the Canadian Museum of Nature,

00:25:12.055 --> 00:25:15.589
their risk profile changed over time.

00:25:15.589 --> 00:25:18.347
They had identified
storage as a major issue.

00:25:18.347 --> 00:25:22.713
Then they made improvements
and mitigated it.

00:25:22.713 --> 00:25:25.880
As to Harvard, well, watch this space.

00:25:29.368 --> 00:25:31.266
I think I'll finish, actually,

00:25:31.266 --> 00:25:33.807
with a couple of paradoxes
and contradictions

00:25:33.807 --> 00:25:35.057
around funding.

00:25:37.754 --> 00:25:39.230
We'll illustrate it with the

00:25:39.230 --> 00:25:41.515
British Library's Centre for Conservation,

00:25:41.515 --> 00:25:45.098
which some of you may
already have visited.

00:25:48.088 --> 00:25:50.288
Preservation is the custodian and champion

00:25:50.288 --> 00:25:53.035
of long termism, and yet, ironically,

00:25:53.035 --> 00:25:55.279
it's often trapped in
short-term decision making

00:25:55.279 --> 00:25:57.019
and funding.

00:25:57.019 --> 00:25:59.169
A nice paradox that I've observed in my

00:25:59.169 --> 00:26:03.575
first few months here
in an endowment culture

00:26:03.575 --> 00:26:05.730
is that a lot of preservation is supported

00:26:05.730 --> 00:26:08.230
by non-core funding and soft endowment

00:26:08.230 --> 00:26:10.616
or fundraised money.

00:26:10.616 --> 00:26:12.695
The good side of this paradox is that

00:26:12.695 --> 00:26:15.850
it does generally seem attractive

00:26:15.850 --> 00:26:18.252
to donors and endowments, but I would

00:26:18.252 --> 00:26:20.904
be very, very interested in your thoughts

00:26:20.904 --> 00:26:22.821
and experience on this.

00:26:23.792 --> 00:26:27.959
Well, at the BL, I encountered
an infuriating paradox.

00:26:30.494 --> 00:26:34.161
When we were fundraising
for the $25 million

00:26:35.313 --> 00:26:37.278
Centre for Conservation, which I

00:26:37.278 --> 00:26:39.845
was very heavily involved
with over nine years,

00:26:39.845 --> 00:26:43.678
from its conception
through to its completion.

00:26:45.573 --> 00:26:47.866
This is the back of the British Library

00:26:47.866 --> 00:26:50.315
and is the Centre for Conservation,

00:26:50.315 --> 00:26:53.188
which is the first new build since

00:26:53.188 --> 00:26:57.188
that St. Pancras building
was completed in 1998.

00:26:58.706 --> 00:27:02.123
There's the Eurostar station at the back.

00:27:04.043 --> 00:27:06.126
This was a major project.

00:27:07.294 --> 00:27:09.355
It included state-of-the-art studios

00:27:09.355 --> 00:27:12.132
for audio and book conservation.

00:27:12.132 --> 00:27:14.450
Fundamental, absolutely fundamental

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:17.917
to the whole concept was public access.

00:27:17.917 --> 00:27:20.283
These studios were designed to have

00:27:20.283 --> 00:27:24.861
weekly behind-the-scenes
tours for the public

00:27:24.861 --> 00:27:27.028
and lectures and seminars.

00:27:28.667 --> 00:27:31.517
The entrance is a permanent exhibition

00:27:31.517 --> 00:27:34.350
about what is inside the building.

00:27:35.227 --> 00:27:37.101
For example, on the right there,

00:27:37.101 --> 00:27:39.386
there's an interactive where you,

00:27:39.386 --> 00:27:42.433
as a visitor, can make decisions about

00:27:42.433 --> 00:27:44.743
how to conserve, I think it was a

00:27:44.743 --> 00:27:46.910
19th-century printed book.

00:27:48.919 --> 00:27:52.534
Or you can preserve a sound recording,

00:27:52.534 --> 00:27:56.701
such as, I think this was
Nelson Mandela's trial tapes.

00:27:59.455 --> 00:28:02.075
You can compare your restored version

00:28:02.075 --> 00:28:05.497
with that of an audio archivist's.

00:28:05.497 --> 00:28:08.838
There was a huge emphasis
on training and development

00:28:08.838 --> 00:28:12.577
of both staff and also of the profession.

00:28:12.577 --> 00:28:15.249
We set up a degree, a foundation degree,

00:28:15.249 --> 00:28:17.945
in conjunction with the
University of the Arts.

00:28:17.945 --> 00:28:19.857
It was sponsored
internships in book, paper

00:28:19.857 --> 00:28:22.753
and audio conservation and preservation.

00:28:22.753 --> 00:28:25.326
This was a high-risk project.

00:28:25.326 --> 00:28:27.491
It was the first capital
fundraising project

00:28:27.491 --> 00:28:29.123
for the British Library.

00:28:29.123 --> 00:28:32.463
The irony was that we could get funding

00:28:32.463 --> 00:28:35.729
for the training, for the internships,

00:28:35.729 --> 00:28:39.918
and we could get funding
for the public programs.

00:28:39.918 --> 00:28:41.737
This exhibition area, we could have sold

00:28:41.737 --> 00:28:45.316
the naming rights five times over.

00:28:45.316 --> 00:28:47.399
But for the conservation,

00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:50.754
we couldn't get funding, as that was seen

00:28:50.754 --> 00:28:53.336
as a core mandate of the British Library.

00:28:53.336 --> 00:28:55.955
Ironically, fundraising for the building

00:28:55.955 --> 00:28:57.659
was challenging, as well, even though

00:28:57.659 --> 00:29:00.042
usually people will support buildings

00:29:00.042 --> 00:29:03.719
and equipment, and not
what goes on in them.

00:29:03.719 --> 00:29:05.893
Yet, the irony was if we
didn't get the funding

00:29:05.893 --> 00:29:07.776
for the building, we
couldn't do the activities.

00:29:07.776 --> 00:29:09.450
We couldn't do the
conservation, the training,

00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:13.200
the public programs that
went on inside them.

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:19.085
Lots of paradoxes, lots of ironies,

00:29:19.085 --> 00:29:21.002
lots of contradictions.

00:29:22.970 --> 00:29:25.970
What are some of the lessons from my

00:29:26.843 --> 00:29:31.283
professional journey from
artisan conservation?

00:29:31.283 --> 00:29:33.189
When Roger Powell, he used to introduce

00:29:33.189 --> 00:29:35.324
all his assistants as fourth generation

00:29:35.324 --> 00:29:36.657
Arts and Crafts.

00:29:38.022 --> 00:29:40.398
I've gone from artisan conservation

00:29:40.398 --> 00:29:44.565
to strategic management in
large-scale research libraries

00:29:45.648 --> 00:29:50.566
in a climate of cloud
computing and crowdsourcing.

00:29:50.566 --> 00:29:53.399
Well, the spectrum of preservation

00:29:54.445 --> 00:29:56.945
has got bigger, broader, wider

00:29:58.102 --> 00:29:59.852
and more complicated.

00:30:02.057 --> 00:30:04.161
From artisan conservation at one end

00:30:04.161 --> 00:30:08.059
of the spectrum, there are
many, many more shades.

00:30:08.059 --> 00:30:10.849
Through a whole rethinking
of environmental parameters

00:30:10.849 --> 00:30:13.562
of storage and energy efficiency,

00:30:13.562 --> 00:30:17.360
through to collections-wide
risk assessment,

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:20.154
through to bitstream preservation,

00:30:20.154 --> 00:30:24.206
emulation and virtual universal computing.

00:30:24.206 --> 00:30:26.301
The spectrum of preservations got bigger,

00:30:26.301 --> 00:30:28.314
broader and wider.

00:30:28.314 --> 00:30:29.907
All this chopping of preservation

00:30:29.907 --> 00:30:31.593
and conservation into little chunks

00:30:31.593 --> 00:30:33.336
of book and paper and photographs

00:30:33.336 --> 00:30:34.976
and audiovisual and digital,

00:30:34.976 --> 00:30:37.151
I don't think it makes sense anymore.

00:30:37.151 --> 00:30:40.705
They're all part of that
continuum of information,

00:30:40.705 --> 00:30:45.243
of information mediums, and
they're all interconnected.

00:30:45.243 --> 00:30:47.642
For those working in the field,

00:30:47.642 --> 00:30:49.309
no job is unchanged.

00:30:50.170 --> 00:30:51.888
Mine has changed exponentially,

00:30:51.888 --> 00:30:54.422
almost beyond recognition.

00:30:54.422 --> 00:30:57.351
No job should be unchanged, and no job

00:30:57.351 --> 00:30:58.851
will be unchanged.

00:30:59.740 --> 00:31:02.821
For those involved with
any aspect of preservation,

00:31:02.821 --> 00:31:05.470
everyone's been touched by these issues

00:31:05.470 --> 00:31:07.576
and should be actively engaged with them

00:31:07.576 --> 00:31:09.909
in order to remain relevant.

00:31:11.278 --> 00:31:13.594
Are the next generation being trained

00:31:13.594 --> 00:31:15.279
with that full spectrum?

00:31:15.279 --> 00:31:18.324
There's usually a time lag.

00:31:18.324 --> 00:31:20.074
Where's my successor?

00:31:21.712 --> 00:31:24.109
Are there enough people in the system

00:31:24.109 --> 00:31:27.359
who already are spanning this spectrum?

00:31:29.132 --> 00:31:32.166
Preservation has changed
and then the nature

00:31:32.166 --> 00:31:35.236
of what's being preserved has changed.

00:31:35.236 --> 00:31:39.847
We still have the legacy
of physical collections

00:31:39.847 --> 00:31:44.827
and digitization has become
a lot more sophisticated.

00:31:44.827 --> 00:31:46.956
As the critical mass grows and projects

00:31:46.956 --> 00:31:50.414
join up, and digital
scholarship mines data

00:31:50.414 --> 00:31:53.863
and text in new ways, that's challenging

00:31:53.863 --> 00:31:56.124
some of the assumptions that underpinned

00:31:56.124 --> 00:31:57.909
preservation as it was practiced

00:31:57.909 --> 00:31:59.742
even a generation ago.

00:32:00.656 --> 00:32:03.309
Now we can use advanced technology to make

00:32:03.309 --> 00:32:07.372
rare objects more accessible, not less.

00:32:07.372 --> 00:32:10.133
Artificially reconstructed objects

00:32:10.133 --> 00:32:14.300
can be more authoritative
than the authentic fragments.

00:32:15.496 --> 00:32:17.792
Coming full circle to my first paradox

00:32:17.792 --> 00:32:20.269
about preservation being about the future

00:32:20.269 --> 00:32:23.798
more than the past, with
these futuristic ventures,

00:32:23.798 --> 00:32:26.465
we can be more true to the past.

00:32:27.691 --> 00:32:30.426
Thank you once again,
Bobby, for inviting me.

00:32:30.426 --> 00:32:32.825
I was very honored to be
asked to give this talk.

00:32:32.825 --> 00:32:34.502
Thank you all for coming.

00:32:34.502 --> 00:32:38.085
I'm extremely honored you all came.

00:32:38.085 --> 00:32:39.830
Thank you for your patience.

